## Test Anti-Pattern Audit — Severity-Ranked Findings

This test suite is a textbook example of **"coverage without confidence"** — every production method is invoked (→ 95% coverage), but almost nothing is actually *verified*.

---

### 🔴 Critical — Tests That Prove Nothing

| # | Test | Anti-Pattern | Bug It Misses |
|---|------|-------------|---------------|
| 1 | `UpdatePrice_Works` (L33) | **Fire-and-forget** — calls `UpdatePrice` but never asserts the price changed. | Price could stay at 9.99m forever; test still green. |
| 2 | `RemoveProduct_Works` (L39) | **No post-condition** — never checks the product is gone. | `Delete` could be a no-op; test still green. |
| 3 | `ApplyBulkDiscount_Works` (L69) | **No verification** — never checks resulting prices. | Discount math could multiply instead of reduce; test still green. |
| 4 | `GetProductCount_Works` (L64) | **Dead result** — `count` is captured but never asserted. | Could return -1; test still green. |
| 5 | `ImportFromCsv_Works` (L85) | **No round-trip check** — never verifies the imported product exists or has correct fields. | Parse errors, wrong column mapping — all invisible. |
| 6 | `AddProduct_Works` (L17) | **Write-only** — adds a product but never reads it back. | Save could silently discard; test still green. |

**Fix pattern for all above — add meaningful assertions:**

```csharp
// Example: UpdatePrice_Works
_catalog.AddProduct(new Product("SKU-001", "Widget", 9.99m));
_catalog.UpdatePrice("SKU-001", 14.99m);
var updated = _catalog.GetProduct("SKU-001");
Assert.AreEqual(14.99m, updated!.Price);

// Example: RemoveProduct_Works
_catalog.AddProduct(new Product("SKU-001", "Widget", 9.99m));
_catalog.RemoveProduct("SKU-001");
Assert.IsNull(_catalog.GetProduct("SKU-001"));

// Example: ApplyBulkDiscount_Works
_catalog.AddProduct(new Product("SKU-001", "Widget", 100m));
_catalog.ApplyBulkDiscount(0.10m);
Assert.AreEqual(90m, _catalog.GetProduct("SKU-001")!.Price);

// Example: GetProductCount_Works
_catalog.AddProduct(new Product("SKU-001", "Widget", 9.99m));
Assert.AreEqual(1, _catalog.GetProductCount());

// Example: ImportFromCsv_Works
_catalog.ImportFromCsv("SKU-001,Widget,9.99");
var p = _catalog.GetProduct("SKU-001");
Assert.AreEqual("Widget", p!.Name);
Assert.AreEqual(9.99m, p.Price);
```

---

### 🟠 High — Weak Assertions

| # | Test | Anti-Pattern | Why It's Dangerous |
|---|------|-------------|-------------------|
| 7 | `GetProduct_Works` (L26) | **Assert.IsNotNull only** — never checks Sku/Name/Price values. | Could return a completely wrong product; test still green. |
| 8 | `SearchProducts_Works` (L47) | **Assert.IsNotNull on a list** — never checks count or content. | Could return an empty list or include "Gadget"; test still green. |
| 9 | `ExportToCsv_Works` (L77) | **Assert.IsNotNull on a string** — never validates CSV format. | Could export garbage like `,,`; test still green. |
| 10 | `GetAllProducts_Works` (L56) | **Assert.IsNotNull on a list** — `GetAll()` can never be null anyway (`ToList()` returns empty). | Assertion is tautological. |

**Fix pattern:**

```csharp
// SearchProducts_Works
var results = _catalog.Search("Widget");
Assert.AreEqual(1, results.Count);
Assert.AreEqual("SKU-001", results[0].Sku);

// ExportToCsv_Works
var csv = _catalog.ExportToCsv();
Assert.AreEqual("SKU-001,Widget,9.99", csv);
```

